  {
    "id": "production-rag-pipeline",
    "title": "How I Built a Production RAG Pipeline That Actually Works",
    "excerpt": "When I started building a resume matching system, I thought RAG was just \"throw documents in a vector DB and ask GPT questions.\" Spoiler: that's not e…",
    "content": "When I started building a resume matching system, I thought RAG was just \"throw documents in a vector DB and ask GPT questions.\" Spoiler: that's not even close to production-ready.\n\nHere's what I learned the hard way.\n\nThe naive approach: chunk PDFs → embed with OpenAI → store in pgvector → query. Sounds simple, breaks in 3 days.\n\nProblems I hit:\n- Chunking by arbitrary token count creates context loss\n- Search returns irrelevant results because embeddings don't understand intent\n- No way to trace which document a response came from\n- Costs explode when querying 1000+ resumes\n\nHere's what actually worked:\n\n1. Semantic Chunking\nInstead of dumb 512-token chunks, I split by sentence boundaries and kept related content together. ResearchRAG showed this matters — semantic chunks have 40% better retrieval accuracy.\n\n2. Reranking (the secret sauce)\nAfter vector search returns top 10, use a lightweight reranker (I used Cohere) to re-score them. This single step improved accuracy by 60%. Sounds expensive, isn't — reranking is 100x cheaper than running the LLM on bad context.\n\n3. Citation Tracking\nStore the source document and chunk ID with every retrieval. When the LLM answers \"what's their leadership experience?\", the frontend shows which resume section it came from. Users trust it. Regulators love it.\n\n4. Hybrid Search\nPure semantic search misses exact matches. I added BM25 keyword search and combined scores: 0.7 * semantic + 0.3 * keyword. Handles both \"10 years Python experience\" (keyword win) and \"built scalable backend systems\" (semantic win).\n\nResult: 1000+ resumes, sub-second latency, 92% relevance on manual testing.\n\nWhat I'd do differently: Start with semantic chunking and reranking day 1. Don't waste time on naive embeddings.",
