  {
    "id": "async-queues-recruitment",
    "title": "Async Task Queues Saved My Recruitment Platform From Burning Money",
    "excerpt": "We launched a recruitment tool that used to sync job postings, screen candidates, and update pipelines in real time. Sounded great. Crashed under load…",
    "content": "We launched a recruitment tool that used to sync job postings, screen candidates, and update pipelines in real time. Sounded great. Crashed under load.\n\nThe problem: every sync operation → API call → wait for response → update database. One job posting = 50 API calls. 10 postings = 500 calls. 100 concurrent users = we're dead.\n\nI moved everything to SQS + async workers.\n\nHow it works now:\n1. Frontend submits job posting\n2. FastAPI validates → publishes to SQS → returns 202 (accepted)\n3. Worker picks up message → calls APIs in parallel → updates DB\n4. Failed? Retry 3x → goes to Dead Letter Queue → alert sent\n\nResult: recruitment cycle time dropped from 6 days to 2.4 days (40% faster). Cost per operation: 1/10th what it was.\n\nThe key insight: users don't need instant responses. They need reliable ones. Async queues let you be both.\n\nWhat I implemented:\n- Idempotency keys (same request twice = same result, not duplicate work)\n- Structured logging (trace every message: which queue, which worker, what failed)\n- DLQ monitoring (automatic alerts if jobs fail 3x)\n\nLesson learned: if your endpoint takes > 1 second, you probably need a queue.",
    "tags": [
      "Backend",
      "Queues",
      "Scaling"
    ],
    "palette": "linear-gradient(135deg,#ff5ea0,#9b7bff)",
