  {
    "id": "realtime-dashboards-websockets",
    "title": "Real-Time Dashboards Without Polling — WebSockets + Redis Pub/Sub",
    "excerpt": "I built an analytics dashboard that used to refresh every 5 seconds via polling. Users hated it. Bandwidth was terrible. Database was getting crushed.",
    "content": "I built an analytics dashboard that used to refresh every 5 seconds via polling. Users hated it. Bandwidth was terrible. Database was getting crushed.\n\nSwitched to WebSockets + Redis pub/sub. Performance changed completely.\n\nHere's the architecture:\n\nOld way (polling):\nBrowser → GET /metrics every 5s → database query → return JSON\nWith 1000 concurrent users = 12,000 queries per minute to the database. That's not scalable.\n\nNew way (pub/sub):\nCelery job: every 30s, compute metrics → publish to Redis channel\nWebSocket handler: subscribed to that channel → instantly fan-out to all clients\n1000 users → 1 database query per 30s → all get updated at the same time\n\nBenefits:\n- 99% less database load\n- Updates happen instantly (not in 5-second increments)\n- Bandwidth drops because we're not sending repeated data\n- Users feel like the dashboard is \"alive\"\n\nWhat I learned:\n- WebSockets aren't overkill for real-time (they're the right tool)\n- Redis pub/sub scales to thousands of subscribers on one machine\n- The hard part isn't the tech, it's handling disconnects gracefully (browser closes, network drops, server restarts)\n\nCode pattern: That one line replaced 12,000 database queries per minute.",
    "tags": [
      "Realtime",
      "WebSockets",
      "Redis"
    ],
    "palette": "linear-gradient(135deg,#5ec8ff,#9b7bff)",
