  {
    "id": "idempotency-keys-production",
    "title": "Idempotency Keys — How I Stopped Creating Duplicate Records",
    "excerpt": "One bug destroyed my day: a user submitted a job posting, connection dropped, they clicked submit again. Now we have two identical job postings in the…",
    "content": "One bug destroyed my day: a user submitted a job posting, connection dropped, they clicked submit again. Now we have two identical job postings in the system.\n\nSimple problem. Surprisingly hard to solve correctly.\n\nThe naive fix: check if posting already exists. Doesn't work because of timing: between checking and inserting, another request came through. Race condition.\n\nThe real solution: Idempotency Keys.\n\nHow it works:\n1. Client sends request with header: `Idempotency-Key: abc-123-def`\n2. Server checks Redis: `GET idempotent:{key}`\n3. If exists → return stored result (we've seen this before)\n4. If not → process request → store result → SET Redis key with TTL 24h\n\nWhy this matters:\n- Exactly once semantics (the holy grail of distributed systems)\n- Works even if your server crashes mid-request (Redis remembers the result)\n- Users can retry forever, always get same outcome\n- No more duplicate records in the database\n\nReal example from my code:\nUser submits recruitment posting with `Idempotency-Key: uuid`\nRequest 1: processes → returns job_id: 123\nRequest 2 (network retried): returns job_id: 123 (same, not 124)\n\nThis single pattern has saved me from so many bugs.\n\nLesson: if your endpoint mutates state, use idempotency keys.",
    "tags": [
      "Backend",
      "Reliability"
    ],
    "palette": "linear-gradient(135deg,#c8ff5e,#ff5ea0)",
