  {
    "id": "agents-that-dont-hallucinate",
    "title": "Building an AI Agent That Doesn't Hallucinate (Mostly)",
    "excerpt": "I built an agent to autonomously screen recruitment candidates. First version: it made up candidates that didn't exist.",
    "content": "I built an agent to autonomously screen recruitment candidates. First version: it made up candidates that didn't exist.\n\nThis is the problem with agents: they're confident liars.\n\nHere's what I learned:\n\nAgent failures come in 3 categories:\n\n1. Hallucination (making stuff up)\nSolution: RAG + citation. Don't let the agent answer without a source document. Every answer must come from an ingested resume or job posting.\n\n2. Infinite loops (agent keeps calling same tool over and over)\nSolution: LangGraph state machine. Define exactly which nodes connect to which. No \"go back to PLAN if confused\" loops. If an agent gets stuck after 3 retries, fail gracefully.\n\n3. Tool misuse (agent calls search with wrong parameters)\nSolution: function calling with strict schemas. Tell GPT: \"when you call search, parameters MUST be {query: string, max_results: int}\". Enforce it.\n\nMy current agent design:\n\nState machine with explicit states:\n- PLAN: break goal into steps (GPT decides what to do)\n- SEARCH: call Tavily (only web search tool available)\n- APPROVAL: pause, ask human \"are you sure about this?\" (for sensitive actions)\n- SUMMARIZE: condense findings\n- SYNTHESIZE: final answer\n\nKey rule: agent CANNOT go backward. No loops. If it fails at SEARCH, it goes to FAILED state, not back to PLAN.\n\nCost tracking:\n- Every step logs token usage\n- Show users: \"this answer cost $0.0043 and took 12 seconds\"\n- Prevents surprise $500 bills\n\nResult: recruited 200+ candidates via automation without a single hallucination that made it to a human.\n\nNot 100% accurate. Good enough for production.",
    "tags": [
      "AI",
      "Agents",
      "LangGraph"
    ],
